 "bug_semantics": "NLTK 3.9.4 introduced nltk.pathsec as a centralized I/O sentinel. pathsec.open() calls validate_path() and, when ENFORCE is True, raises PermissionError for paths outside allowed NLTK data roots before delegating to builtins.open(). StreamBackedCorpusView in nltk/corpus/reader/util.py accepts a raw string fileid and, in _open(), still calls builtins.open(self._fileid, \"rb\") (and SeekableUnicodeStreamReader on that handle). That path never hits validate_path, so an attacker who can supply fileid (web/API/multi-tenant NLP pipeline) can read arbitrary local files such as /etc/passwd even with ENFORCE=True. XMLCorpusView subclasses StreamBackedCorpusView, so the same _open sink applies. The content-read is the open() branch; os.stat on the string fileid leaks size/existence only.",
