 "bug_semantics": "GitPython's GitConfigParser._write.write_section interpolates the in-memory section name as a raw git-config header via fp.write((\"[%s]\\n\" % name).encode(...)). Repo.config_writer().set_value(section, option, value) (and add_section/set/add_value/rename_section) stored that name without rejecting CR, LF, or NUL. An attacker-controlled section such as \"user]\\n[core\" is therefore serialized as two valid headers, [user] then [core], so a following option like hooksPath=/tmp/evil_hooks becomes core.hooksPath in .git/config. Git honors that path; a later commit/merge/checkout runs hooks from the attacker directory (RCE). This is a residual bypass of the 3.1.49 value-only guard, which blocked the same outcome when newlines were smuggled in the value argument (indented [core] continuation) but left the section/option names unsanitized.",
