 "bug_semantics": "Parse Server's default fileUpload.fileExtensions control is supposed to stop uploads that browsers will render as active content. FilesRouter.createHandler prefers the filename extension when the name contains a dot, and only consults Content-Type as a fallback. Independently, FilesController.createFile sets contentType = mime.getType(filename) || contentType, so an unrecognized extension (note.foo, data.bar, or no usable extension) stores the client Content-Type verbatim. A malformed Content-Type that is not a type/subtype token (image, image/, image//svg+xml) does not yield a blocklisted subtype: split('/')[1] is empty or the bare token image is not in the blocklist, so the upload is accepted. S3/GCS/Azure then serve that unparseable Content-Type; browsers MIME-sniff an HTML/script body and execute it in the application origin against other users who open the file URL. GridFS is not a sink because it recomputes Content-Type from the filename and sends X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff. Later GHSA-v8x7-r927-cc93 added a Content-Type subtype check when mime does not recognize the extension, but still used the same split('/') extraction, so malformed values continued to bypass.",
