  "bug_semantics": "_estimate_batch_cost looks up the model in the hardcoded _costs.PRICING table and, on a miss, uses {} so .get('output', 0.0) is 0.0. _check_batch_reservation then treats reserved_usd <= 0 as a no-op and returns without comparing remaining daily/monthly headroom. That reservation is the only pre-fan-out guard for helper_call_parallel / delegate_parallel: each worker otherwise re-reads the same pre-batch on-disk cost total and can individually pass while the batch collectively overruns cost_guardrails daily_cap_usd/monthly_cap_usd. An operator using an unpriced id (Ollama/vLLM/self-hosted/new SKU) or a steered parallel-helper model argument therefore believes the cap is enforced when a single parallel batch can spend without a reservation. The first-write even documented this as intended: 'Unknown models return 0 — can't estimate, so we don't block.'",
