 "bug_semantics": "Netty codec-dns encodes and decodes DNS domain names without RFC 1035 size/content checks. On encode, a user-influenced name is split on '.' and each label's Java length is written as a single length byte followed by the raw bytes (ByteBufUtil.writeAscii / getBytes), so a NUL in a label is emitted, a label >63 can write 0xC0-0xFF (compression-pointer range), an empty label historically terminated or truncated the rest of the name, and the total encoded name can exceed 255 octets. On decode, each non-pointer length byte is trusted: the decoder only checks that that many bytes exist, then appends them to an unbounded StringBuilder, so a malicious DNS response can produce labels >63 and decoded names >255. Downstream resolvers, caches, and allowlists that assume RFC 1035 names can then disagree with Netty (cache poisoning / validation bypass) or spend unbounded memory. The 2026 fix rejects empty interior labels, labels >63, embedded NULs, and names >255 on encode, and throws TooLongFrameException for decoder label >63 or decoded name >255.",
